					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/norimori" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1222161); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1222161&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608671057" alt="NoriMori" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1222161)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/norimori" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1222161); return false;" >NoriMori</a></span> <span class="odate time_1321215341 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Nov 2011 20:15</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1304966">
									

<p>That's what I thought too, about the water. In fact, he shouldn't even have been able to get to the door with all that water rushing in. That's pretty much all that bothers me, aside from the last entry, where an agent successfully used it in an infiltration mission. If it opens in a <em>random</em> door, how would that help him? Did he just keep opening it until he got where he wanted?</p>
<p>I really like this though, it reminds me of The Lost Room. Has anyone here seen that? It's a miniseries, with (I think) three 2-hour episodes. The Lost Room and the Objects associated with it would make quintessential SCPs, e.g. a pencil that creates a 1961 US penny when you tap the end of it on a hard surface; a bus ticket that transports any person whose skin it touches to U.S. Route 66 outside Gallup, New Mexico (just near the motel where the Lost Room is); a comb that stops time for ten subjective seconds when run through the user's hair; a nail file that induces brief unconsciousness in anyone who has light reflected from it into their eyes; etc. Hell, if the Room and the Objects were SCPs, the Cabals in the miniseries could be Groups of Interest. The Lost Room is like its own little SCP database just waiting to be written. :P</p>

							</div>

								
					
